  "title": "Bias-Attributed Calibration: Decomposing Confounded SAE Features into Competing Cognitive Bias Mechanisms for Interpretable LLM Calibration",
  "core_problem": "Existing per-instance LLM calibration methods (CORAL, ATS, PING, CCPS) and SAE-based steering methods (CorrSteer) correct miscalibration or improve accuracy without explaining WHY a given instance is miscalibrated. CorrSteer identifies SAE features correlated with binary correctness but treats correctness as monolithic — it cannot distinguish whether a wrong answer stems from choice-supportive overconfidence or from evidence-overweighting underconfidence. Critically, Patel & Aina (arXiv 2604.19974) have now shown that CorrSteer-style correctness-correlated features are a heterogeneous mixture of three functionally distinct populations — pure uncertainty features (essential for accuracy), pure incorrectness features (functionally inert despite statistical significance), and confounded features (encoding both signals, causally relevant) — yet even this three-way decomposition stops at the uncertainty-vs-correctness axis and does not explain which cognitive bias mechanism drives each instance's miscalibration. Kumaran et al. (Nature MI, 2026) identified at least two competing cognitive biases underlying LLM miscalibration (choice-supportive bias vs. contradictory-evidence overweighting), but no method attributes per-instance calibration errors to specific bias mechanisms or provides interpretable, bias-typed corrections operating on the causally relevant feature subpopulation.",
  "opportunity": "Three concurrent advances create an untried synthesis. First, Patel & Aina (arXiv 2604.19974, April 2026) demonstrated a functional dissociation within SAE features: pure incorrectness features are 'functionally inert' (near-zero accuracy change when suppressed), while confounded features encoding both uncertainty and correctness are 'causally relevant' — just 3 confounded features from a single mid-network layer predict correctness with AUROC ~0.79, enabling selective abstention that raises accuracy from 62% to 81% at 53% coverage on Llama-3.1-8B. This resolves CORAL's critique (Miao et al., ICML 2026) that 'individual SAE features capture narrow semantic concepts correlated with correctness but do not encode a coherent correctness direction' — CORAL is correct about the majority of correctness-correlated features (which are indeed inert), but confounded features are the causally active exception. Second, Kumaran et al. (Nature MI, 2026) identified that LLM miscalibration results from two mechanistically distinct biases pulling in opposite directions: choice-supportive bias inflates confidence in selected answers, while contradictory-evidence overweighting causes excessive confidence deflation. Third, Miao & Ungar (arXiv 2603.25052, March 2026) revealed that calibration and verbalized confidence are orthogonal linear directions within model representations, with the Reasoning Contamination Effect showing these directions can actively oppose each other — implying that cognitive bias features may project differentially onto these orthogonal confidence subspaces. The untried combination: applying 2604.19974's functional triage to isolate the causally relevant confounded feature population, then decomposing those confounded features by Kumaran et al.'s cognitive bias taxonomy, enabling bias-type-specific steering that avoids both the inert-feature dilution problem (CORAL's critique) and the opposing-bias cancellation problem (BAC's hypothesis).",
  "landscape": "The LLM calibration landscape has moved well beyond uniform temperature scaling, with concurrent advances in SAE-based steering, per-instance calibration, and mechanistic confidence analysis creating a rich but fragmented field. **SAE-based steering**: CorrSteer (Cho et al., ICML 2026, arXiv 2508.12535) selects ~25 SAE features by Pearson correlation with binary correctness labels, achieving +3.3% MMLU accuracy on Gemma-2-2B via activation addition with decoder columns. CorrSteer does not report calibration metrics; as evaluated by Miao et al. (CORAL) on Deepseek-LLM-7B-Chat, CorrSteer achieves 22.01% ECE versus 22.12% baseline — a statistically insignificant improvement. Key limitations: static steering (same direction regardless of input), binary correctness label conflates bias types, poor task transfer. **SAE feature functional dissociation**: Patel & Aina (arXiv 2604.19974, April 2026) use pre-trained SAEs (LlamaScope, Gemma Scope) to partition correctness-correlated features into three functionally distinct populations via a 2x2 quadrant framework (confidence x correctness): pure uncertainty features are 'functionally essential' (suppression degrades accuracy up to -10.2%); pure incorrectness features are 'functionally inert' (mean accuracy change ~0.03% despite statistical significance); confounded features encoding both signals are 'detrimental' — targeted suppression yields +1.1% accuracy and 75% entropy reduction, with effects transferring to ARC-Challenge and RACE. This establishes that correctness-correlated features are not a monolithic population, and only confounded features have meaningful causal effects on model behavior. **Per-instance activation-based calibration methods**: CORAL (Miao et al., ICML 2026) uses weight-decay MLP probes on internal activations for inference-time steering, achieving ~50% ECE reduction and ~10% accuracy improvement. CORAL's Section 5.2 ablation finds individual SAE features produce negligible causal effects on calibration (mean ECE change 1.25x10^-4), arguing that effective steering requires aggregating distributed signals via regularized probes rather than sparse feature selection — a critique that applies to CorrSteer's full feature set but not necessarily to the confounded subpopulation identified by 2604.19974. PING (medRxiv 2025) achieves up to 96% ECE reduction via hidden-state probing. CCPS (EMNLP 2025) achieves ~55% ECE reduction via representational stability under adversarial perturbation. Adaptive Temperature Scaling (Xie et al., EMNLP 2024) improves calibration 10-50%. BaseCal (2026) reduces ECE ~43% via base-model geometry projection. **Confidence geometry**: Miao & Ungar ('Closing the Confidence-Faithfulness Gap', arXiv 2603.25052, March 2026) demonstrate that calibration (internal accuracy estimates) and verbalized confidence are encoded as orthogonal linear directions (cosine similarity <0.04 across layers), with a 'Reasoning Contamination Effect' where joint reasoning-and-rating causes the confidence direction to actively oppose the correctness direction. Their two-stage adaptive steering pipeline achieves dramatic ECE reductions (14.9 to 3.7 on MATH for Llama-3.1-8B). This orthogonality implies cognitive bias features may project differentially onto calibration vs. verbalized-confidence subspaces. **Confidence component decomposition**: Tsujimura & Tagade ('LLM Assertiveness can be Mechanistically Decomposed', arXiv 2508.17182, August 2025) decompose assertiveness/overconfidence into orthogonal emotional and logical sub-components using residual activations in Llama 3.2, with distinct causal effects from steering each component — establishing that confidence-related behaviors have multi-component structure amenable to independent steering. **Training-based methods**: Rewarding Doubt (ICLR 2026) fine-tunes for calibrated confidence via RL. **Prompt-based methods**: SteerConf (NeurIPS 2025) uses multi-level steering prompts. CaliDist (2026) measures behavioral robustness to semantic distractions. **Regime discovery**: Kobalczyk & van der Schaar (arXiv 2605.13484, May 2026) learn calibration-aware representations revealing input-dependent miscalibration heterogeneity across 12 LLMs, but do not attribute regimes to bias mechanisms. **Mechanistic overconfidence work**: 'Wired for Overconfidence' (arXiv 2604.01457) identifies compact confidence-inflation circuits and stratifies incorrect predictions by truth-injection response. **Bias mechanism identification**: Kumaran et al. (Nature MI, 2026) identified choice-supportive bias and contradictory-evidence overweighting as competing behavioral biases but proposed no correction framework. Zhuang et al. (arXiv 2512.03082) address choice-supportive bias via fine-tuning but do not disentangle at the representation level. **Representation-level bias separation**: 'Sycophancy Is Not One Thing' (arXiv 2509.21305) demonstrates distinct sycophantic behaviors map to separable linear directions, independently steerable, with probe-to-TruthfulQA transfer. Ji et al. and Joshi et al. (EMNLP 2025) find single calibration directions capturing only net effects. **SAE reliability**: 'Do SAEs Generalize?' (arXiv 2502.19964) shows inconsistent cross-distribution transfer, motivating explicit validation. 'What Drives Representation Steering?' (arXiv 2604.08524) finds >90% circuit overlap between steering methods at same layers, cautioning shared substrate. **Pre-trained SAE availability**: LlamaScope provides 256 SAEs for Llama-3.1-8B (32K/128K widths); Qwen-Scope provides SAEs for Qwen3-8B (16x expansion). **Gap**: No existing method (1) operates on the causally relevant confounded feature subpopulation identified by 2604.19974 rather than the full correctness-correlated set, (2) decomposes confounded features into cognitive bias types from Kumaran et al.'s taxonomy, (3) provides per-instance bias attribution, or (4) applies bias-type-specific steering informed by the calibration-confidence orthogonality revealed by 2603.25052.",
  "inspired_by": "Gap: CorrSteer (ICML 2026) demonstrates SAE-based steering but achieves negligible calibration improvement (as evaluated by CORAL on Deepseek-LLM-7B-Chat). Patel & Aina (2604.19974) explain part of this: CorrSteer's feature set includes functionally inert incorrectness features that dilute the steering signal. But even restricting to their confounded features (which DO have causal effects), uniform suppression yields only +1.1% accuracy — suggesting the confounded population itself contains features with opposing calibration effects. Pattern 1: Behavioral bias separation maps to representational separability ('Sycophancy Is Not One Thing'; Tsujimura & Tagade's emotional/logical assertiveness decomposition). Pattern 2: Calibration and verbalized confidence are orthogonal directions (Miao & Ungar 2603.25052), implying bias features may project differently onto each. Pattern 3: Confounded features are the causally active subpopulation within correctness-correlated SAE features (2604.19974), and represent the right substrate for further decomposition. Pattern 4: CorrSteer's accuracy improvement (+3.3%) proves SAE features DO encode behaviorally relevant information — the ECE failure is specifically a calibration-directionality problem. Untried combination: Hierarchical decomposition — first isolate confounded features via 2604.19974's framework (removing inert dilution), then decompose confounded features by Kumaran et al.'s cognitive bias types (preventing bias-typed cancellation) + per-instance continuous attribution + bias-type-specific activation steering informed by confidence geometry.",
  "approach": "We propose Bias-Attributed Calibration (BAC), a hierarchical framework that first isolates causally relevant SAE features via functional triage, then decomposes them into cognitive bias types — choice-supportive bias vs. contradictory-evidence overweighting — enabling interpretable, bias-typed calibration corrections. BAC addresses two compounding problems in CorrSteer-style steering: inert-feature dilution (CORAL's critique, validated by 2604.19974) and opposing-bias cancellation (BAC's core hypothesis). BAC proceeds in five stages, leveraging pre-trained SAE suites (LlamaScope for Llama-3.1-8B, Qwen-Scope for Qwen3-8B). **Stage 0 — Functional feature triage (building on 2604.19974)**: We reproduce Patel & Aina's 2x2 quadrant framework (confidence x correctness) using their entropy-threshold methodology to partition SAE features into pure uncertainty, pure incorrectness, and confounded populations on our evaluation benchmarks. This serves as a gating stage: only confounded features — those encoding both uncertainty and correctness signals, which 2604.19974 demonstrated have genuine causal effects (+1.1% accuracy, 75% entropy reduction from just 3 features) — proceed to bias-type decomposition. Pure incorrectness features (functionally inert per 2604.19974) and pure uncertainty features (essential for accuracy, not bias-related) are excluded. This directly addresses CORAL's critique that 'individual SAE features capture narrow semantic concepts' — we agree, and filter to the subpopulation where this critique does not apply. **Stage 0.5 — Diagnostic: cancellation vs. narrowness test**: Before proceeding with bias decomposition, we run an early-stage diagnostic to distinguish our cancellation hypothesis from CORAL's narrowness explanation within the confounded population. We partition natural evaluation instances into overconfident (high confidence + incorrect) and underconfident (low confidence + correct) subsets, then measure whether confounded features show bimodal activation patterns across these subsets. Bimodal activation (different confounded features activating on overconfident vs. underconfident instances) supports the cancellation hypothesis; uniform activation supports the narrowness explanation. If the diagnostic supports narrowness (uniform activation with no bias-typed structure), we report this as a negative result and fall back to CORAL-style aggregation of confounded features only. If the diagnostic supports cancellation (bimodal patterns), we proceed with bias-typed decomposition. This makes BAC informative regardless of outcome. **Stage 1 — Controlled bias-isolation datasets with transfer validation**: We construct contrastive probe sets isolating each bias mechanism with confound control. For choice-supportive bias: paired prompts where models evaluate identical answers presented as self-generated vs. externally-provided, with paraphrase-controlled variants. For evidence overweighting: prompts with graded contradictory evidence strength on questions where the model's initial answer is correct. Transfer validation: we verify that SAE features identified via contrastive probes also activate differentially on natural instances where biases manifest behaviorally — operationalized as sticking behavior for choice-supportive bias (maintaining confidence despite counter-evidence) and disproportionate confidence collapse for evidence-overweighting (excessive deflation from mild contradiction). Supported by 'Sycophancy Is Not One Thing' demonstrating synthetic-to-TruthfulQA direction transfer, and 2604.19974 showing confounded features transfer across MMLU, ARC-Challenge, and RACE. **Stage 2 — Bias-typed decomposition of confounded features**: Within the confounded feature population from Stage 0, we identify bias-associated subsets via differential activation analysis against Stage 1 probes. The key analysis: do confounded features decompose into bias-typed clusters, or do individual confounded features encode both biases? We additionally analyze whether bias-typed confounded features project differentially onto the orthogonal calibration vs. verbalized-confidence directions identified by Miao & Ungar (2603.25052) — choice-supportive features may primarily affect the verbalized-confidence direction (inflating expressed confidence), while evidence-overweighting features may primarily affect the calibration direction (disrupting internal accuracy estimates). This geometric analysis grounds bias attribution in the known confidence geometry. **Stage 3 — Causal validation with continuous-attribution fallback**: We validate via causal intervention: ablating choice-supportive confounded features should reduce choice-supportive bias without substantially affecting evidence sensitivity, and vice versa. Because both biases relate to confidence modulation and may share computational substrate (as cautioned by 'What Drives Representation Steering?' finding >90% steering circuit overlap, and as suggested by the emotional/logical assertiveness overlap in Tsujimura & Tagade 2508.17182), we adopt a continuous attribution framework. If cross-talk exceeds 20%, we employ a per-instance regressor predicting continuous bias mixture vectors [alpha_csb, alpha_eow] in [0,1]^2 (with alpha_csb + alpha_eow <= 1, remainder attributed to bias-independent miscalibration). The separability degree itself becomes a reported finding. **Stage 4 — Bias-typed activation steering**: At inference time, BAC diagnoses the bias mixture within the confounded feature population and applies proportionally weighted modulation: dampening choice-supportive confounded features proportional to alpha_csb (reducing overconfidence from answer commitment) and attenuating evidence-overweighting confounded features proportional to alpha_eow (preventing excessive confidence deflation). Unlike CorrSteer which steers a mixture of inert, essential, and opposing-bias features (explaining its accuracy gain but ECE stagnation), BAC's hierarchical decomposition avoids both inert-feature dilution (Stage 0 filtering) and bias-typed cancellation (Stage 2-4 decomposed steering). Unlike CORAL which aggregates all distributed signals without interpretability, BAC provides an interpretable diagnosis: 'this instance is miscalibrated primarily due to choice-supportive bias (alpha_csb=0.78) acting through confounded features that project onto the verbalized-confidence direction.'",
