  "description": "CorrSteer (ICML 2026) selects SAE features correlated with binary correctness for inference-time steering, achieving +3.3% MMLU accuracy but negligible calibration improvement (22.01% vs. 22.12% ECE, as evaluated by Miao et al. (CORAL) on Deepseek-LLM-7B-Chat — CorrSteer itself does not report ECE). Two competing explanations exist for this ECE failure: CORAL argues individual SAE features are too narrow and distributed for coherent steering (mean ECE change from single feature ablation: 1.25x10^-4); we hypothesize that CorrSteer's feature set conflates opposing bias types whose calibration effects cancel. Patel & Aina (arXiv 2604.19974) resolve part of this puzzle: CorrSteer's correctness-correlated features include functionally inert 'pure incorrectness' features (near-zero accuracy change when suppressed) alongside causally relevant 'confounded' features encoding both uncertainty and correctness (3 features achieve AUROC ~0.79 for correctness prediction). We propose Bias-Attributed Calibration (BAC), a hierarchical framework that synthesizes these insights: (0) functional triage isolates the confounded feature subpopulation, removing inert features that dilute the steering signal (addressing CORAL's critique); (0.5) a cancellation-vs-narrowness diagnostic measures whether confounded features show bimodal activation on overconfident vs. underconfident instances — bimodal patterns support bias-typed decomposition, uniform patterns would constitute an informative negative result; (1) constructs confound-controlled contrastive datasets isolating choice-supportive bias and evidence-overweighting, with explicit transfer validation on natural behavioral instances; (2) decomposes confounded features into bias-typed components using pre-trained SAEs (LlamaScope for Llama-3.1-8B, Qwen-Scope for Qwen3-8B), additionally analyzing projections onto the orthogonal calibration-vs-verbalized-confidence directions discovered by Miao & Ungar (arXiv 2603.25052); (3) validates causal distinctness with a continuous-attribution fallback — if cross-talk exceeds 20%, BAC uses continuous bias mixture vectors [alpha_csb, alpha_eow] rather than hard classification, with separability degree reported as a finding; (4) applies proportionally weighted bias-typed steering within the confounded population, avoiding both inert-feature dilution and opposing-bias cancellation. Building on precedents for multi-component confidence decomposition ('Sycophancy Is Not One Thing' proving separable behavioral directions that transfer to natural inputs; Tsujimura & Tagade 2508.17182 decomposing assertiveness into orthogonal emotional/logical components; 'Wired for Overconfidence' showing circuit-level overconfidence subtypes), BAC is the first framework providing hierarchical, bias-attributed calibration that operates on the demonstrated causally relevant feature subpopulation. We evaluate on MMLU, TriviaQA, PopQA, and NQOpen across Llama-3.1-8B and Qwen3-8B, targeting: (a) ECE reduction competitive with per-instance SOTA (CORAL's ~50%, CCPS's ~55%), with superior performance on bias-heterogeneous subsets; (b) cancellation diagnostic distinguishing bias-typed structure from uniform narrowness within confounded features; (c) geometric analysis of how bias-typed features project onto the calibration-confidence orthogonal subspaces; (d) >75% accuracy on continuous bias-type attribution validated by causal intervention.",
